  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "If the operation \\(x*y\\) is defined by \\(x*y=(x+1)(y+1)-1,\\) then which one of the following is false?",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(x*y=y*x\\) for all real \\(x\\) and \\(y.\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(x*(y+z)\\) equals \\((x*y)+(x*z)\\) for all real\n\\(x,\\) \\(y,\\) and \\(z.\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\((x-1)*(x+1)\\) equals \\((x*x)-1\\) for all real \\(x.\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(x*0=x\\) for all real \\(x.\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(x*(y*z)=(x*y)*z\\) for all real \\(x,\\) \\(y,\\) and \\(z.\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "b",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1982-p7.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Since \\(x*y=x+y+xy,\\) the operation is commutative, has identity \\(0,\\) and becomes ordinary multiplication after adding \\(1.\\) Thus it is associative, and direct expansion also verifies C. Statement B fails when \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=z=0:\\) its left side is \\(1*0=1,\\) but its right side is \\((1*0)+(1*0)=2.\\) Hence B is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "First simplify the operation to \\(x+y+xy\\)",
        "Test the proposed distributive law with \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=z=0\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
